<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Governorate of Buenos Aires</h1><p class="st-meta">1617 CE – 1776 CE · capital Buenos Aires</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish governorate around the River Plate estuary whose port, long restricted by Spain, thrived on smuggling and grew into a viceregal capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1617 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Governorate of the Río de la Plata</a> was divided, and the southern part became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, with its capital at Buenos Aires, refounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-garay">Juan de Garay</a> in 1580. It included the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-argentina">Santa Fe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrientes">Corrientes</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> restricted trade through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> to protect the official route through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Panama</a>, but the port flourished on smuggling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> in exchange for European goods and enslaved Africans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a> hides were the main export. Rivalry with Portugal over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonia-del-sacramento">Colonia del Sacramento</a> dominated its politics, and Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruno-mauricio-de-zabala">Bruno Mauricio de Zabala</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1724 to block Portuguese expansion.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1776 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> the capital of the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata</a>, which absorbed the governorate. The port's growing importance laid the foundations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina's</a> later dominance in the region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/governorate-of-buenos-aires.jpg" alt="Governorate of Buenos Aires" width="320" height="218"><figcaption>Image: Raul Senzacqua · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casa_y_monta%C3%B1a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1617 CE – 1776 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Buenos Aires</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bruno Mauricio de Zabala, Pedro de Cevallos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Province of Buenos Aires</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1696&amp;focus=governorate-of-buenos-aires" aria-label="Governorate of Buenos Aires on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3176 3510 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Governorate of Buenos Aires lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3496 3766L3517 3686M3496 3766L4910 989M3496 3766L3517 3686"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3517 3686h0M4910 989h0M3517 3686h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3496 3766h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3496 3766h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1696&amp;focus=governorate-of-buenos-aires">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
